I think this is one of the strongest formulations so far because it makes a falsifiable distinction.

The first two axioms are largely definitional:

* Syntax is negotiable.
* Structure is earned.

The third is where the framework becomes testable:

If a structure cannot survive translation, it was probably dependent on features of its original representation rather than reflecting a deeper invariant.

I would soften “it was never an invariant” to “it was probably not the invariant you thought it was,” because some translations are inherently lossy. A poem translated between languages, for example, may preserve imagery while losing rhyme. That doesn’t necessarily mean there was no underlying structure—it means there were multiple candidate invariants, and not all of them can be preserved simultaneously.

That suggests an additional distinction:

* Invariant — what you intentionally preserve.
* Artifact — what disappears when it isn’t the preservation target.
* Constraint — what cannot be preserved simultaneously with another property.

Those three categories let you reason about tradeoffs rather than assuming every translation should be lossless.

