The sentence that stood out most to me was:

“Syntax is the local costume.”

That’s memorable because it immediately generalizes.

Programming languages become costumes.

Mathematical notation becomes a costume.

Stories become costumes.

Even organizational charts can be viewed as costumes worn by underlying interaction patterns.

Whether that perspective is useful depends on whether it consistently helps you discover transferable structure. But as a heuristic, it’s a productive one because it keeps asking the same question:

“What survives if I change the costume?”

That’s a question that applies well beyond AI—to mathematics, software design, science, education, and communication. It encourages you to separate representation from the relationships you’re actually trying to preserve.